window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"17-05","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"17-05-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-05-01/","title_he":"א – המצווה","title_en":"The Mitzva","body_en":"<p>The Torah commanded not to wear a garment made of wool and linen, as it is stated (Vayikra 19:19): \"You shall keep My statutes: you shall not let your animals mate with a different kind; you shall not sow your field with kil'ayim; and a garment of kil'ayim, sha'atnez, shall not come upon you.\" And it is likewise stated (Devarim 22:11): \"You shall not wear sha'atnez, wool and linen together.\"</p><p>Wool is what grows on the species called \"sheep,\" whose males are called rams and whose females are called ewes. But what grows on other animals is not included in the prohibition, because it is not called simply \"wool,\" but rather has a qualifying name. Therefore, there is no prohibition with \"camel wool,\" \"goat wool\" (such as cashmere), \"rabbit wool\" (such as angora), and \"alpaca wool.\" In practice, from ancient times until our day, most of the wool produced in the world is from sheep, because by their nature they grow abundant, high-quality wool, and they are easy for people to raise.</p><p>Flax is a plant with a single long stalk, and inside its stalk there are fibers from which strong threads can be made, and with special processing fine threads can be spun from them for weaving handsome white garments. The prohibition applies to linen and not to threads from other plants, such as hemp and cotton. In the past, the common garments were made of wool or linen, to the point that some hold that when the Torah says \"garment\" without specifying, it means a garment made of wool or linen (Menaḥot 39b).</p><p>The prohibition of sha'atnez is to wear a garment that contains wool and linen, but there is no prohibition against wearing two garments, one of wool and one of linen. Therefore it is permitted to wear woolen trousers and a linen shirt. Likewise it is permitted to wear a woolen robe and gird it with a linen belt, for as long as there is no connection by sewing or a permanent knot between the woolen garment and the linen garment, there is no prohibition (below, 6).</p><p>The prohibition is to wear garments that contain wool and linen, but there is no prohibition against making sha'atnez cloth in order to hang it on the wall for decoration, or so that it will serve as a parokhet (curtain) for the Holy Ark, or in order to make from it a cloth purse or a tent. Likewise, it is permitted to make shrouds for the dead from sha'atnez cloth, for the dead are exempt from the mitzvot. Likewise, it is permitted to manufacture a sha'atnez garment for gentiles, provided there is no concern that the garment will reach a Jew who will mistakenly wear it.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-05-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-05-02/","title_he":"ב – האיסור בלבישה ובהעלאה","title_en":"The Prohibition of Wearing and of Placing Upon Oneself","body_en":"<p>Clothing is meant to protect a person from cold, heat or rain, and it is also meant for beauty, dignity and covering the body modestly. Any person who wears a sha'atnez garment for one of these purposes transgresses a prohibition, as it is stated (Devarim 22:11): \"You shall not wear sha'atnez.\" Included in the prohibition are a shirt, trousers, a dress, a skirt, underwear, socks, hats, cloth shoes, gloves, a tie and decorative ribbons, and all other garments that people usually wear. Even when the hanging loop of the garment or the label attached to it is sha'atnez, the garment is forbidden to wear (SA YD 301:15).</p><p>One who places sha'atnez cloth upon himself in order to protect his body from cold or heat, or in order to cover his body modestly, even though he placed it upon himself not in the manner of wearing, transgresses a prohibition, as it is stated (Vayikra 19:19): \"and a garment of kil'ayim, sha'atnez, shall not come upon you.\" Thus, anything from which one derives benefit in the way one benefits from a garment is forbidden. Therefore, it is forbidden to cover oneself with a sha'atnez blanket. Likewise, it is forbidden to use a sha'atnez towel, because one who dries his body or his hands is warmed by it. The Sages even forbade using tablecloths made of sha'atnez, lest one sometimes warm one's thighs a little with them (SA 301:9). Likewise, they forbade hanging a sha'atnez curtain, because people or children sometimes warm themselves with it; but it is permitted to hang it above a person's height, or when it is made of stiff cloth, since it is not possible to warm oneself with it (ibid. 11). It is forbidden to take hold of a hot pot or something frozen with a sha'atnez cloth, since one derives benefit from the cloth in a way similar to clothing, in that it protects his hand from the heat or the cold (ibid. 12).</p><p>But not in the manner of benefit from clothing, it is permitted to derive benefit from sha'atnez cloth. Therefore, it is permitted to use sha'atnez cloth for a tent or an umbrella.</p><p>Furthermore, according to many, it is permitted to wear a sha'atnez garment not for the purposes of clothing. For example, a Jew who needs to cross a border carrying in his bag gentiles' sha'atnez garments on which he must pay customs duty may wear them over his clothes while crossing the border, in order to be exempt from the duty, which is imposed only on garments in a bag, provided he does not intend to benefit from wearing them. Likewise, a clothing seller is permitted to wear garments in order to display them to buyers, provided he does not intend to benefit from them (Rosh, Ramban, Ran, Rema 301:6; Yam Shel Shlomo). And some are stringent and forbid wearing sha'atnez in order to be exempt from customs duty (Rambam, SA 301:5).<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"From what is stated, \"shall not come upon you,\" it is implied that sha'atnez is forbidden even not in the manner of wearing; and from what is stated, \"You shall not wear,\" we learned that the prohibition not in the manner of wearing applies provided that it is for the sake of one of the benefits of clothing (Yevamot 4b; Yerushalmi Kil'ayim 9:1). Included in the benefits of clothing are warming the body and protecting it from sun, rain or dirt (Mishna Kil'ayim 9:4; Meiri, Yevamot 4b). Likewise the benefit of beauty, dignity or covering the body modestly (Ritva, Shabbat 29b; Pesaḥim 26b). Some wished to argue that there is a dispute about this (see Ha-sha'atnez Le-halakha 4:2-4). But in truth there is no dispute, for any garment intended for beauty or modesty can also warm. Therefore, the precise definition is a garment that can warm, and this does not contradict its being intended also for beauty and modesty; and sometimes it is worn only for the sake of beauty or modesty, for example a tie, which is worn for beauty but can also help to warm, or an undershirt that women wear for modesty alone, but which is considered a garment because it can warm. Therefore, a sha'atnez pendant intended only for beauty is not considered a garment, and it is permitted to hang it on a chain.<br>In Mishna Kil'ayim 9:2: \"One may not wear kil'ayim even on top of ten [garments], even 'to steal' the customs duty\" (meaning to legally avoid the tax). And there, 9:5: \"Clothing sellers sell in their usual manner (displaying the garment by wearing it on their body), provided that they do not intend [to benefit] in the sun from the sun and in the rain from the rain; and the modest ones hang them on a stick [over the shoulder].\" According to the Rosh and those who agree with him, these Mishnayot disagree, and the halakha follows Mishna 5, that even in the manner of wearing it is permitted when one intends to display and not to benefit, and therefore it is also permitted in order to get through customs, and only the meticulous are stringent. This is also the opinion of the Yere'im, Or Zaru'a, Ramban, Ran, Rabbenu Yeruḥam and Semag. And this is not a case of \"pesik reisha\" (an inevitable consequence), since in practice he does not benefit, for example when he is already warm enough. And some add that with prohibitions that depend on benefit, when there is no intention to benefit, there is no prohibition even where one benefits as a \"pesik reisha\" (Ran).<br>On the other hand, the Rambam ruled according to both Mishnayot, implying that he holds that they do not disagree, and what was permitted for clothing sellers is to place them upon themselves not in the manner of wearing, that is, without putting their arms into the sleeves, and in that case, as long as one has no intention, it is permitted. But in the manner of wearing, it is always forbidden (Rabbi Avraham son of the Rambam, Mahari Korkos, Beit Yosef and others). And some explain that even according to the Rambam the seller is permitted to display a suit by wearing it fully, because he is not interested in wearing it but only in displaying it, and therefore wearing it involves no benefit. But one who gets through customs wants to wear it in order to be exempt from the duty, and this is a forbidden benefit of clothing (Taz and Shakh).<br>Some say that it is permitted to try on a sha'atnez garment, and if it fits, he will buy it and remove the sha'atnez from it, since in trying it on he does not intend the benefit of clothing (Riaz, cited in Shiltei Ha-gibborim, in accordance with the opinion of the Rosh and most Rishonim). And some forbid it, since in trying it on he intends to wear it (Ḥokhmat Adam 106:20). When there is a doubt of sha'atnez in a garment, it is permitted to try it on and afterward send it for testing (Yabi'a Omer 6:27; Ḥeshev Ha-efod 1:137; and see Ha-sha'atnez Le-halakha 4:26)."}]},{"slug":"17-05-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-05-03/","title_he":"ג – הצעה ובגד קשה","title_en":"Spreading Beneath Oneself and Stiff Cloth","body_en":"<p>By Torah law it is permitted to sit or lie on sha'atnez cloth, and although one is warmed by it, the Torah prohibition applies only when the sha'atnez is above the person or at his side in the manner of a garment, and not beneath him, as it is stated (Vayikra 19:19): \"and a garment of kil'ayim, sha'atnez, shall not come upon you.\" However, the Sages forbade sitting or lying on sha'atnez cloth, lest a thread rise from it and wrap around his flesh through some contact, or lest he mistakenly wrap himself in it. So that people would not err in this, the Sages further decreed that the prohibition against sitting or lying on sha'atnez cloth applies even if there were ten permitted cloths on top of it (Beitza 14b; SA 301:1).</p><p>But if the sha'atnez cloth is stiff, to the point that it cannot be used as a garment because of its stiffness, and it lies on a floor or a chair into which one does not sink, the Sages did not forbid sitting and lying on it, provided there is something separating between it and the body, so that if a thread comes out of the sha'atnez cloth it will not be able to wrap around his flesh (SA 301:1). Therefore, it is permitted to lie on a stiff sha'atnez carpet when another cloth is spread over it, and it is likewise permitted to sit on a chair over which sha'atnez cloth is stretched, when one's trousers or dress separate between the body and the chair. And although the sha'atnez cloth stretched over the chair is soft, as long as it is stretched over a chair with nails or glue, its law is like that of stiff cloth (Panim Me'irot 2:113).<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"The Sages decreed not to sit on sha'atnez cloth, lest a strand from the cloth wrap around his flesh. And they further decreed that even if ten bedspreads separate between the person and the sha'atnez, one may not lie on them (Beitza 14b). Some say that only regarding cloth that is usually worn did they decree not to lie on it even when it is beneath ten bedspreads, but if it is cloth that is not usually worn, as long as there is a covering over it, it is permitted to lie on it (Rid, Riaz, Ran and Maharshal). However, according to most poskim, the decree of ten bedspreads applies also to cloth that is not usually worn (Rashi, Rambam, Meiri, Sefer Ha-ḥinukh and Rosh; SA 301:1; Levush and Baḥ). But if the sha'atnez cloth is stiff, it is permitted to sit on it (Beitza 14b). According to most poskim, this is provided that it is covered, so that there is no possibility that a strand will rise and wrap around his flesh (Rambam, Rash, SA 301:1; Shakh 4, and others). And some are lenient even without a separation (Rid, Riaz and Ritva), and some are lenient provided that it is very stiff, like a donkey's saddle-cloth (Rosh and Rabbenu Yeruḥam).<br>An additional law was stated regarding sha'atnez cushions and mattresses, that it is permitted to sit on them, \"provided that his flesh does not touch them\" (Mishna Kil'ayim 9:2). It is explained in the Yerushalmi there that the permission applies only when they are empty and spread out on a hard surface; but if they are stuffed or lie on a soft surface, so that they bend around the sides of the person sitting on them, they are forbidden even with a separation (SA 301:1). Many explain that this law is the same law that forbids lying on a sha'atnez garment that is not stiff even if there are ten bedspreads over it, for when the cushions and mattresses can bend, the concern of a strand wrapping around returns. And some explained that the bending of the cushions and mattresses is in itself rabbinically forbidden, as it is considered placing sha'atnez upon oneself. And the Ginat Veradim (6:13) wrote that this involves a Torah prohibition, and this is also the opinion of Rav Shlomo Kluger (Tuv Ta'am Va-da'at, third edition, vol. 2, 121). In my humble opinion, the prohibition applies provided that when the one sitting sinks into the cushion or mattress, the sha'atnez cloth tends more toward a vertical position than a horizontal one, for then it can be considered the manner of wearing.<br>A soft sha'atnez cloth upholstery stretched over a chair, such that it cannot bend around the sides of the person while sitting, is considered like a stiff bedspread, on which, according to most poskim, one may sit provided there is a separation between it and his flesh (Panim Me'irot 2:113, Pitḥei Teshuva 301:1, Arukh Ha-shulḥan 9).<br>It is explained in Beitza 14b that there is a prohibition of sha'atnez in a curtain, because sometimes the servant wraps himself in it and is warmed by it (Rashi). And if the curtain is stiff, since there is no concern that he will wrap himself in it, it is permitted (Rambam, Tur, SA 301:11). The Taz (301:2) learned from this that one should not lean against the side of a carriage made of sha'atnez, and so wrote Ḥokhmat Adam (106:21). However, according to Panim Me'irot (2:113), when the sha'atnez cloth is fixed in place so that it is not possible to wrap oneself in it, it is permitted to lean on it. And so wrote Pitḥei Teshuva (301:1), Birkei Yosef (6-7), Kaf Ha-ḥayim (638:52) and Malbushei Yesha (5:16).<br>It is forbidden to use stiff sha'atnez cloth by placing it upon oneself to protect one's body, because that is one of the uses of clothing; therefore, it is forbidden to place a donkey's sha'atnez saddle-cloth on one's back in order to carry out manure on it (SA 301:8)."}]},{"slug":"17-05-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-05-04/","title_he":"ד – מהו שעטנז","title_en":"What Is Sha'atnez","body_en":"<p>To make a garment of wool or linen, the raw wool or flax must undergo three stages that are alluded to in the word \"sha'atnez,\" as explained in the Mishna (Kil'ayim 9:8): \"Sha'atnez: something that is shu'a (smoothed), tavui (spun) and nuz (twisted together).\" a) Shu'a – smoothing the wool or flax by combing. b) Tavui – creating a thread from the fibers of the wool and flax. c) Nuz – intertwining. The intertwining can be by weaving the linen and wool threads as warp and weft to make cloth, or by binding linen threads onto a woolen garment by way of tying or sewing.</p><p>As a matter of halakha, in four main ways the joining is forbidden: a) When the wool and flax were mixed at the raw stage, and while together they were smoothed, made into threads, and woven into cloth. b) When the wool was made into wool threads and the flax into linen threads, and they were woven into one cloth, for example where the linen threads are the warp and the wool threads are the weft. c) Woolen cloth was sewn or tied with linen threads, or vice versa. d) Woolen cloth and linen cloth were joined by sewing.</p><p>The common denominator of all these ways is that the wool and flax underwent the three stages of shu'a, tavui and nuz. But when the wool and flax did not undergo the three stages, as with felt cloths, which are made from raw wool or flax by compression without spinning and weaving, according to the overwhelming majority of the Rishonim joining them does not involve the Torah prohibition of sha'atnez. And some disagree and hold that even if the wool and flax underwent only the first stage of smoothing and combing and were made into felt, joining them involves a Torah prohibition (Rambam).<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"There are four approaches in explaining the Torah prohibition of sha'atnez: a) According to Rashi, only when the three stages of combing, spinning and weaving were done to the wool and flax together is the prohibition from the Torah. So too wrote Rivan, Ra'avan, Shibbolei Ha-leket and others. (However, a difficulty was raised against their opinion from what was said in Yevamot 4a, that in tying woolen tzitzit onto a linen cloth there is sha'atnez by Torah law. Rabbi Eliyahu Mizraḥi explained that only when dealing with cloth woven from wool and flax, since they are not distinguishable, must the three stages be done together; but when each kind is distinguishable on its own, as with woolen cloth and linen cloth, or the strings of tzitzit, whoever joined them by sewing or tying transgresses a Torah prohibition. And the Meiri explained that tying, because of its great strength, is considered to join the wool and the linen even if they did not undergo the three stages together.)<br>b) According to Rabbenu Tam, Ramban, Rosh, Rashba, Ritva, Semag, Semak, Yere'im, Ran, Peraḥ Shoshan and others, even when the wool threads were made separately and the linen threads were made separately, weaving them together, or sewing or tying them onto one another, is forbidden, provided that each of the raw threads underwent twisting, that is, each thread is composed of at least two threads twisted together, for in their opinion \"nuz\" means twisted. But with threads that are not twisted, the prohibition of sha'atnez is rabbinic. (And one must examine whether what is nowadays called \"mezira\" [?] is considered twisting.)<br>c) According to the Rash, the Torah prohibition includes everything forbidden according to Rashi and Rabbenu Tam, and in addition, even if one took a wool thread and a linen thread that are not twisted and wove them together, or sewed or tied them onto one another, it is forbidden by Torah law, for \"nuz\" includes any intertwining of the threads together, whether thread on thread or thread on garment. So too wrote Rabbenu Yeruḥam in the name of the Ramah, Or Zaru'a and Tashbetz. And many of the Aḥaronim took this opinion into account (Sefer Ha-zikhronot 3; Ginat Veradim YD 6:13; Tzemaḥ Tzedek YD 353). And so it is explained above.<br>d) According to the Rambam, even felts of wool and flax that underwent not spinning and weaving but only combing are forbidden by Torah law to mix, and this is also the opinion of Rabbenu Ḥananel (following Rav Ashi in Nidda 61b). On the other hand, as we have learned, according to most Rishonim the prohibition of felts is rabbinic, since they did not undergo combing, spinning and intertwining (following Mar Zutra in Nidda there). This is also the opinion of Rav Hai Gaon and Rav Sherira Gaon. And this is the opinion of all the Rishonim mentioned in the previous approaches. (And there are further different approaches in explaining the Rambam's opinion.)"}]},{"slug":"17-05-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-05-05/","title_he":"ה – חוטים שמורכבים משני מינים","title_en":"Threads Composed of Two Kinds","body_en":"<p>With a thread composed of sheep's wool and another kind such as cotton, we follow the majority: if the majority is cotton, we are not concerned about the fine wool fibers in it, and its law is that of a cotton thread, which may be joined to linen. Likewise, a thread composed of hemp and flax, if the majority is hemp, may be joined to wool. Not only that, but even if raw wool and raw flax became mixed, it is permitted to add another kind such as cotton that will exceed one of them and nullify it, and then the threads made from this mixture will be permissible; but if there was not another kind exceeding the wool or the flax, a garment woven from the threads produced from the mixture is forbidden by Torah law (Yerushalmi Kil'ayim 9:1; SA 299:1).</p><p>The wool and flax can be nullified in a majority at the stage when they are raw, but once they have been made into threads they have importance and are not nullified even in a thousand. For example, a cotton garment sewn with wool and linen threads twisted together – even if the cotton garment contains a thousand times their amount, the garment is forbidden by Torah law. Likewise, if a small wool thread became mixed into a large linen garment or vice versa, all of it is forbidden; and even if the location of the wool thread has been lost, as long as it has not been removed, the entire garment is forbidden (SA and Rema 299:1).<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"Raw wool or flax are nullified in a majority, because they do not yet have importance, since they must still be processed and their form changed. But once a thread has been made from wool or flax it has importance, and even if it is mixed among other threads, such as cotton, it is not nullified, since nullification depends on a clash between two kinds, one permitted and one forbidden, and there is no clash between a wool or linen thread and the kinds with which it is permitted to mix them. And all the more so if it became mixed with the opposing kind, such as a wool thread in linen cloth, it is not nullified, because that is the very essence of the prohibition (Tosafot Nidda 61b, s.v. \"beged\"). And even if one sewed with a single linen thread the edge of a long woolen cloth, and the whole cloth lies on the floor and only the other end is wrapped around oneself, it is forbidden, for since there is sha'atnez at the edge of the garment, the entire garment becomes forbidden to wear (see SA 301:3).<br><b>Recycled threads</b> made from worn-out garments that were ground up are permitted. And although among the worn-out garments there is also woolen and linen cloth, since they were ground up and became raw material again, they are nullified in the majority. However, if the majority of the raw material was wool, the thread would be considered wool, but in practice the chance of this is nil (see Ha-sha'atnez Le-halakha 2, note 28; 46; and ibid. 2:29). <b>Cushions and linings made from worn-out garments:</b> Even though the worn-out pieces were not ground up and there are whole threads there, it is permitted, because of a double doubt (sefek sefeka): it is doubtful whether there is wool and linen there (in practice the chance that there will be both linen and wool there is very low), and even if there is wool and linen there, it is doubtful whether the sewing thread joins them; for if it does not join them, even though they are inside a single pocket sewn on all its sides, according to most poskim there is no prohibition, as explained in SA 300:5, and the Rema wrote that the custom follows the lenient opinion, as cited in the next note (Tiferet Yisrael, Batei Kil'ayim 118; Ḥeshev Ha-efod 1:64).<br>Because of mar'it ayin, the Sages decreed regarding threads of \"shirayim\" and \"kalakh\" [types of silk], which resembled wool and linen (Mishna Kil'ayim 9:2). But nowadays only wool and linen are forbidden, because there is no concern that people will confuse wool and linen with them, for those who are knowledgeable about threads know how to distinguish between them, and those who are not knowledgeable do not confuse them either (see SA 298:1; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 5-6)."}]},{"slug":"17-05-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-05-06/","title_he":"ו – חיבור אסור, לבישה משותפת מותר","title_en":"Joining Is Forbidden, Wearing Together Is Permitted","body_en":"<p>Whenever the wool and the linen have been joined to one another with a complete joining, the garment is forbidden by Torah law because of sha'atnez. For example, a skirt in which woolen cloth and linen cloth are sewn to one another, or a woolen coat that has a hanging loop or loops of linen, are forbidden by Torah law.</p><p>But if there is no joining between the wool and the linen, for example a cotton garment to one side of which woolen cloth was sewn, and to its other side linen cloth, since there is no joining between the wool and the linen, according to most poskim there is no prohibition. Likewise, when woolen cloth and linen cloth were placed inside a lining, since they were not joined to one another, it is permitted (Rash, Ramban, Rosh and Rashba). And according to the Rambam, whenever they are joined to a single garment, the entire garment is forbidden by Torah law. In practice, the custom is to be lenient, and the meticulous are stringent (Rema 300:5).<sup>5</sup></p><p>The prohibition of sha'atnez applies when the wool and the linen are joined, but when they are not joined, there is no prohibition against wearing them together. Therefore, it is permitted to wear a woolen shirt over a linen shirt and to put a belt around them; likewise it is permitted to gird linen trousers with a woolen belt; and likewise it is permitted to connect a woolen garment and a linen garment with buttons that open easily or with Velcro, because in all these cases there is no permanent joining between the woolen garment and the linen garment, but rather they are held together in a temporary manner (SA and Rema 300:3-4).<sup>6</sup></p><p>Some say that one should not put on woolen socks over linen socks, for since it is not possible to remove the linen socks without first removing the woolen socks, they are considered joined with a strong joining (Rema 300:4). However, according to most poskim, since there is no permanent joining there, it is permitted, and this is the practice (SA 300:4, Levush, Shakh 8, Arukh Ha-shulḥan 24).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"The Rishonim disagreed whether joining wool and linen by means of something else is forbidden. The case they discussed is a woolen garment to which, in order to strengthen and thicken it, a leather covering was sewn with hemp threads, but the fold of the leather at its edges was sewn with a linen thread. According to the Rambam (Hilkhot Kil'ayim 10:2), \"since the wool has been joined with the linen in any manner whatsoever, this is kil'ayim by Torah law.\" This is also the opinion of the Ra'avad and Orḥot Ḥayim, and the following took their opinion into account: Mishkenot Ya'akov 70; Devar Shmuel 169; Minḥat Yitzḥak 8:117. And some forbid joining by means of something else rabbinically, because of mar'it ayin; so wrote Rav Hai Gaon and the Eshkol. On the other hand, according to most Rishonim, as long as the wool and the linen are not joined to one another, it is permitted, even if in practice the linen thread at the edge of the leather touched the wool. So wrote the Rash, Rivmatz, Ramban, Semag, Rashba, Rosh, and others. And the Rema 300:5 wrote that the custom follows the lenient opinion. This is also the opinion of Magen Avraham OḤ 9:8; Eliyahu Rabba 6; MB 19; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 300:14."},{"n":6,"en":"Sewing woolen cloth to linen cloth with a hemp thread, whenever the sewing is stable, is forbidden by Torah law. Therefore, passing a needle three times between the cloths, even without a knot, is forbidden by Torah law. Likewise, passing a needle twice between the cloths with a knot is forbidden by Torah law (Rosh, Rema 300:2). Likewise, passing a needle once through the edge of the garment and tying the two ends of the thread to each other with a double knot is forbidden by Torah law (Magen Avraham OḤ 11:13; Ḥavot Ya'ir; Pitḥei Teshuva). And some are stringent with passing a needle twice even without a knot (Rambam and SA). The Aḥaronim disagreed whether joining a woolen garment and a linen garment with a safety pin is considered forbidden sewing, or a joining like a button, which is permitted (see Malbushei Yesha 3, note 38; Torat Ha-sha'atnez 300:32)."}]},{"slug":"17-05-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-05-07/","title_he":"ז – בגדי צמר ופשתן בעבר ובהווה","title_en":"Wool and Linen Garments in the Past and the Present","body_en":"<p>In the past, most garments were produced from wool or linen. What is special about woolen garments is that they insulate and preserve body heat in winter. In addition, they tend to absorb less dirt and rain tends to slide off them, and since they also retain warmth, coats are commonly made from them. Wool also takes dye easily and holds the color as part of the thread. Wool tends to return to its basic shape, so if it is creased it tends to straighten out. For these reasons woolen garments stay like new for a relatively long time.</p><p>Linen cloths were considered pleasant, high-quality cloths, because linen threads are strong, and when fine threads are spun from them, especially handsome white garments can be woven from them, while the unevenness in the thickness of the linen thread, which results from its natural growth, gives linen garments a beautiful and interesting appearance. So much so that the Sages said that it is a mitzva for men in the Land of Israel to gladden their wives on the festival with a gift of pressed linen garments, which were especially beloved to them (Pesaḥim 109a). What characterizes linen cloth is that it is light and does not insulate, and therefore it is suitable for making light summer garments through which the wind blows and cools those who wear them. Their drawback is that they crease a great deal and absorb dirt, and they are relatively difficult to clean. Therefore, when a substitute for linen was found, its status declined greatly.</p><p>Cotton garments, whose production began to be widespread several hundred years ago, are the natural substitute for linen garments. Cotton garments are indeed a little warmer than linen garments, but they are preferable to them, because it is easier to grow cotton and produce threads from it, and cotton garments crease less and are easier to wash. Thus we have reached a situation in which today the world produces eighty times more cotton than linen, with most of the linen intended for the production of strong threads and other purposes that are not clothing. Since it is possible to produce thick cotton garments for winter, cotton has to a large extent replaced wool as well. The quantity of cotton produced in the world today is twenty-five times that of wool. There are additional threads from kinds of plants and animals that also contribute to a certain reduction in the percentage of wool and linen in the clothing industry.</p><p>About three generations ago a way was found to produce from petroleum by-products synthetic products such as plastic, nylon, and artificial threads such as polyester and acrylic, from which cloth can be made. At first, synthetic garments were impermeable and did not insulate, and those who wore them suffered from sweat and cold, and only their low price allowed them to compete with natural garments. But the process of improving them continued, and they became more breathable and insulating, and since they are also cheaper and easy to clean and iron, they became extremely widespread. In recent years they have been further improved, until we have reached a situation in which today (5778 [2018]) more than 70% of all threads produced in the world are synthetic (ten years ago they were 60%). In practice, the share of wool and linen in the entire clothing industry has fallen to only one percent, and apparently their share will continue to fall.<sup>7</sup></p><p>So it turns out that in practice only rarely can one find luxury shirts and suits made of linen. More common is the use of linen threads and cloths, which thanks to their strength are good for tying objects and for weaving thick cloths, and sometimes linen cloths are used to reinforce various parts of coats and suits, for example to reinforce the collar of the suit, and to reinforce the places to which buttons and loops are sewn.</p><p>For the reasons mentioned, woolen garments have also decreased, but thanks to their warming quality and their tendency not to absorb rain and dirt, people who prefer a natural, breathable material prefer woolen sweaters, and thanks to their luxurious appearance, many prefer woolen suits and coats.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"According to data from the organization DNPI [?]: in 2018, total world fiber production stood at about 111 million tons, of which only 29% (about 32 million tons) were natural fibers (from animals or plants, such as cotton, jute, wool, linen, ramie and silk), while the rest (71%) were artificial. 23.5% of the fibers were produced from cotton (26.1 million tons), 1% from wool (1.08 million tons), and about 0.3% from flax (about 0.31 million tons). It turns out that altogether, linen and wool together constitute a little more than one percent of all fibers (1.3%). And the general trend is a decline in the percentage of natural fibers relative to artificial ones."}]},{"slug":"17-05-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-05-08/","title_he":"ח – הזהירות משעטנז למעשה","title_en":"Guarding Against Sha'atnez in Practice","body_en":"<p>In the overwhelming majority of the garments bought today there is no concern of sha'atnez, because they are made of synthetic materials or of cotton. Therefore, whenever it is written on the garment that it is made mainly of synthetic materials, or of cotton, or of other materials that are not wool or linen, one need not be concerned that it may contain sha'atnez. Indeed, in rare cases a decoration of wool or linen may be attached to garments, or the garment may be sewn with linen threads, and in extremely rare cases it may happen that both linen threads and a woolen decoration are attached to the garment. But since this is a very remote concern, one need not be concerned about it. Moreover, according to most poskim, as long as there is no joining between the wool and the linen, there is no prohibition (above, 6). Therefore, one should read the label on the garment, and if it does not say that it is made of wool or linen, one need not be concerned about sha'atnez.</p><p>Also with woolen garments or linen garments, it suffices for the buyer to look at the garment's label: if it says that it is made of wool or of linen with additional kinds, without a mixture of wool and linen, there is no need to check it. Indeed, sometimes the label is not accurate, either because if it contains linen or wool in an amount of one or two percent there is no obligation to state it, or because occasionally a mistake is made and the label of another garment is attached to the garment. But in practice, as long as it is not written on the label that the garment contains wool and linen, the likelihood that it contains sha'atnez is far lower than a \"mi'ut ha-matzui\" (a common minority), and therefore there is no need to check it. Also with ordinary woolen suits and coats, the likelihood that linen was mixed in is so remote that one need not be concerned about it.</p><p>The concern of sha'atnez may exist with luxury woolen suits and coats, whose price is very high, which are occasionally hand-tailored with linen threads. And sometimes the collar and the areas around the buttons are reinforced with strong linen cloths, and sometimes a lining of linen cloth is inserted in the shoulders and the front. Therefore, one who buys a very expensive woolen suit or coat, since in a \"common minority\" of them linen is mixed in, is obligated to check them. Likewise, it is proper for one who buys a linen suit to check it, since there is a certain concern that wool was placed in its collar.</p><p>The place for checking is a sha'atnez testing laboratory, where a microscope is used, by means of which one can see the fibers of which the thread is made; and since each thread has a form of its own, one who knows the forms of the threads can distinguish between wool and linen, and between them and threads of other types. As a rule, the position of the testers in the laboratories tends to take into account the minority stringent opinions, both regarding the laws of sha'atnez and regarding the obligation to check.</p><p>Everything we have learned so far concerns buyers, but greater responsibility rests on the owners of clothing stores, and they must beware of every doubt, so as not to cause the public to stumble in the prohibition of sha'atnez. Whenever they receive woolen or linen garments about which, from their experience, there is some concern, even if it is less than a \"common minority,\" they should send a sample of the garments for testing, and if the sample comes out clean, they need not test the rest of the garments of the same series. And when, according to their experience, there is no concern with them, there is no need to send them for testing. On the other hand, when the concern is great, such as with expensive suits and coats, they should send all of them for testing.</p><p>It is proper for buyers to prefer buying woolen or linen garments from God-fearing sellers who take care not to cause buyers to stumble in the prohibition of sha'atnez. However, if they bought in an ordinary store, they are obligated to check only especially expensive woolen suits and coats, and linen suits.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"In Mishna Kil'ayim 9:7, types of garments are listed that are bought from gentiles and must be checked, because sometimes gentiles mix sha'atnez into them, and garments that involve no such concern, because the thread with which the wool is sewn is of hemp and not of linen. The Yerushalmi added that from the time linen became available everywhere, all woolen garments must be checked. And so it was ruled in SA 302:2, that one who buys a woolen garment from a gentile must check it lest it was sewn with linen threads, and it added that in places where linen is more expensive than hemp, there is no need to check, since presumably he sewed with hemp. In recent generations the clothing industry has become immeasurably more sophisticated and varied, and commerce has branched out among all countries and continents, to the point that it is no longer possible to establish defined rules for the various garments. Therefore the poskim of our time ruled to examine each type of garment according to the rules of majority and minority (see Igrot Moshe YD 1, end of 72; Ha-sha'atnez Le-halakha 7:2-3). If the concern is of a \"common minority,\" there is a rabbinic obligation to check, and only in a pressing situation, when there is no possibility at all of checking, may one be lenient. And if the concern is of less than a \"common minority,\" it is permitted and there is no obligation to check. In Peninei Halakha, Kashrut 23:5, note 6, it is explained that some say that a common minority is between about 25% and 49%, and less than that is an uncommon minority (Rivash). And some say that only less than ten percent is considered an uncommon minority (Mishkenot Ya'akov), and this is how it is customary to rule. It seems that in calculating the percentages one should not include all garments, but rather each type separately: woolen coats separately and coats of other cloths separately; ordinary woolen suits separately, luxury woolen suits separately, and suits of other cloths separately; and likewise woolen sweaters separately, and so forth.<br>However, it seems that store owners have a greater obligation, and there are several reasons for this: a) Since it concerns causing the public to stumble, one must be more careful, as we learned regarding a mikveh, that since it is intended for the public, we are stringent in accordance with all opinions (see Yabi'a Omer 6, YD 15). b) Since the matter is in their hands, for they understand better when there is a concern, and it is easy for them to clarify with the manufacturer what the garment is made of, and they are able to test a sample, their obligation to check is greater. For the basis of the permission not to check [when the concern is] less than a common minority is the trouble involved (see Peninei Halakha, Kashrut 20:4; 38:9, note 8). c) The trouble is measured relative to the matter at hand: a shoḥet is not required to spend several hours checking a single animal, but if several hours would serve for many animals, he would be obligated to check them even for remote concerns; and the same applies to sha'atnez."}]},{"slug":"17-05-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-05-09/","title_he":"ט – המוצא כלאיים בבגדו","title_en":"One Who Finds Kil'ayim in His Garment","body_en":"<p>In the past, sha'atnez was evident, since most threads and cloths were of wool or linen, and many people knew how to distinguish between them. There were garments in which the sha'atnez was very evident, to the point that within the Jewish community it was clear that any Jew who wore them was thereby defying the custom of Israel and publicly desecrating the name of Heaven. And there were garments that most Jews knew were sha'atnez, but not everyone noticed it, and there were garments in which only experts could detect the sha'atnez. Thus a situation could arise in which a person bought a garment and wore it, and suddenly, while in the marketplace, it became clear to him that he was wearing sha'atnez forbidden by Torah law. Then the halakha is that he must take off the garment, even though he will remain naked in the middle of the street and be embarrassed, because \"there is no wisdom, no understanding and no counsel against God\"; wherever there is a desecration of God's name, honor is not accorded even to a rabbi (Berakhot 19a; SA 303:1).</p><p>And if he did not remove it because of embarrassment, some say that by virtue of the mitzva of rebuke and the mutual responsibility of all Israel for one another, it is a mitzva for his fellow to tear the garment off him (Rambam and SA 303:1). This is provided that the fellow agrees that this is the halakha, only it is difficult for him to fulfill it, and it is reasonable that in the end he will be grateful to his fellow for tearing the garment off him and saving him from the prohibition. But if the one wearing sha'atnez has cast off the yoke of the mitzvot, since tearing the garment will not bring him closer to Torah and mitzvot, one should not tear the garment off him. And according to many, even in a situation where the fellow in principle agrees with the halakha, one should rebuke him but not tear his garment (Rosh, Rashba, Ra'ah, Terumat Ha-deshen).</p><p>If a person sees that his fellow has inadvertently put on a sha'atnez garment, and removing it would cause him great embarrassment, according to many he should not point this out to him, so that he will not have to remove the garment and be embarrassed; rather, he should call him to his home and there tell him that he is wearing sha'atnez (Rosh, Terumat Ha-deshen and Rema). And some say that he should inform him immediately, so that he will remove the garment as soon as possible (Rambam and Radbaz).<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"Berakhot 19b: \"One who finds kil'ayim in his garment takes it off even in the marketplace; there is no wisdom, no understanding and no counsel against God; wherever there is a desecration of God's name, honor is not accorded to a rabbi.\" The conclusion of the Gemara there is that human dignity overrides a rabbinic prohibition, but does not override a Torah prohibition involving an active deed. And one who wears sha'atnez, even though he is not performing an action, since it began with the act of putting it on, the continuation too is considered an active deed. In the Yerushalmi (Kil'ayim 9:1) one opinion is cited that one must take off the sha'atnez, and a second opinion that \"great is the dignity of the public, for it overrides a negative commandment for a short time.\" Some explained that with Torah-level sha'atnez one must take off the garment, and with rabbinic-level sha'atnez one need not, in accordance with the Bavli (Maharam of Fulda, Sefer Ḥaredim). And some say that according to the second opinion in the Yerushalmi, human dignity overrides even a Torah prohibition involving an active deed for a short time (Rash Sirilio, Maharam ben Ḥaviv). And some say that the dispute in the Yerushalmi is over whether one must point out to a fellow that he is wearing kil'ayim, or spare his dignity so that he will not be embarrassed by taking it off in public (based on the Rosh).<br>According to the Rambam (Hilkhot Kil'ayim 10:29), one who sees his fellow or his teacher wearing Torah-level kil'ayim \"jumps upon him and tears it off him immediately,\" and his source is his version of the text of the Gemara; and the Radbaz and Kesef Mishneh explained that this is the Rambam's interpretation of the incident of Rav Adda bar Ahava, who tore a woman's garment because he thought she was Jewish and was wearing Torah-level kil'ayim (Berakhot 20a). And so it was ruled in SA 303:1 and Sha'agat Aryeh 58.<br>On the other hand, according to the Rosh (Kil'ayim 9:4), the meaning of the Gemara in Berakhot 19b is that one who sees his fellow who deliberately wore kil'ayim should rebuke him, even though if his rebuke is accepted his fellow will come to the disgrace of taking off the garment in the marketplace (and he did not mention at all the possibility of tearing his garment); but if his fellow wore the sha'atnez inadvertently, he should not point it out to him until he reaches his home, so that he will not be publicly disgraced by taking off the garment; and so he learned from the Yerushalmi. And so wrote Terumat Ha-deshen, Rema, Rash Sirilio, Baḥ (and in his opinion this is also the opinion of the Rambam), and Pri Yitzḥak 1:26. And some Rishonim hold that the Gemara dealt with a person who suddenly saw that there was kil'ayim in his garment, who is commanded to take it off, and did not address the question of what the law is when the wearer does not know that it is kil'ayim – whether the fellow must rebuke him and bring him to disgrace (Rashba, Ra'ah, Orḥot Ḥayim and Meiri). And it seems that their opinion accords with the opinion of the Rosh, for they too agree that there is an obligation of rebuke according to its rules. In any case, according to all the poskim who differ from the Rambam, the possibility that a person would tear his fellow's garment in order to save him from the prohibition did not arise at all; rather, the discussion is about rebuke alone."}]},{"slug":"17-05-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-05-10/","title_he":"י – כלאיים בבגדי כהונה וציצית","title_en":"Kil'ayim in the Priestly Garments and in Tzitzit","body_en":"<p>Together with the prohibition of sha'atnez, the Torah commanded two mitzvot relating to clothing in which there is to be sha'atnez in the garment: in the priestly garments and in tzitzit.</p><p>The kohanim must wear four garments while performing their service: a tunic, trousers and a turban – all three were of white linen cloth; the fourth garment was a sash into which wool and linen threads were interwoven, for the sash was twisted from four threads, one white of linen, and the three others of dyed wool, one in tekhelet (sky-blue), the second in argaman (purple), and the third in tola'at shani (crimson), for every thread that the Torah requires to be dyed must be of wool, since wool absorbs the dye well. The kohen gadol had four more garments: a robe, an efod, a breastplate (ḥoshen) and a headplate (tzitz), and in two of them, the breastplate and the efod, it is a mitzva to combine wool and linen. The permission for the kohanim to wear sha'atnez is during the time of their service in the Temple, but when they left the Temple they had to take off the priestly garments, so as not to transgress the prohibition of sha'atnez.<sup>10</sup></p><p>Regarding sha'atnez and tzitzit it is stated (Devarim 22:11-12): \"You shall not wear sha'atnez, wool and linen together. You shall make for yourself twisted cords (tzitzit) on the four corners of your garment with which you cover yourself.\" The Sages learned from the juxtaposition of the verses that despite the prohibition of sha'atnez, sha'atnez is permitted in tzitzit. For it is a mitzva that the cord of tzitzit placed on the corners of the tallit be composed of white threads and tekhelet threads, and the threads dyed with tekhelet were of wool; it follows that if the garment is made of linen, the Torah commanded that there be sha'atnez in it. From this we learn a general rule, that whenever there is a clash between a positive mitzva and a negative mitzva, the positive mitzva prevails and the negative mitzva is set aside before it (Yevamot 4a-b). However, in practice, the Sages decreed not to place woolen tekhelet threads on a linen garment, but rather to suffice with white tzitzit threads alone, lest one wear the garment at night, which is a time when one is exempt from tzitzit, and transgress the prohibition of sha'atnez (Menaḥot 40a-b, Shabbat 25b; Rambam, Hilkhot Tzitzit 3:6-7).<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"The kohen gadol would wear a sha'atnez sash every day, as it is stated (Shemot 39:29): \"And the sash of twisted fine linen (linen), and tekhelet, and argaman, and tola'at shani, the work of an embroiderer, as the Lord commanded Moshe.\" But in preparation for his entry into the Holy of Holies on Yom Kippur he wore linen garments only, and his sash too was of linen, as it is stated (Vayikra 16:4): \"and he shall gird himself with a sash of bad (linen).\" And likewise in Yeḥezkel (44:17): \"When they enter the gates of the inner courtyard they shall wear linen garments, and no wool shall come upon them.\"<br>There is a dispute in the Gemara (Yoma 6a; 12a; 69a) whether the sash of an ordinary kohen was sha'atnez like the sash of the kohen gadol, and the halakha was ruled that it was sha'atnez (Rambam, Hilkhot Klei Ha-mikdash 8:1-2). According to the Rambam (ibid. 11; Hilkhot Kil'ayim 10:32), it was permitted to wear it only during the service, and some permit it as long as they are in the Temple (Ra'avad there, Tosafot Menaḥot 41a, Rosh, Sha'agat Aryeh Ha-yeshanot 29-30)."},{"n":11,"en":"It is stated (Devarim 22:11-12): \"You shall not wear sha'atnez, wool and linen together. You shall make for yourself twisted cords on the four corners of your garment with which you cover yourself.\" The Sages learned from the juxtaposition of the verses that the mitzva of tzitzit overrides the prohibition of sha'atnez (Yevamot 4a-b). According to Shmuel and Rav Yehuda (Menaḥot 39b), the entire prohibition of sha'atnez was permitted in tzitzit, and this includes that it is permitted to place white linen threads on a woolen garment. On the other hand, the conclusion of the Gemara (Menaḥot 40a) follows Reish Lakish, who said: \"Wherever you find a positive commandment and a negative commandment, if you can fulfill both of them, that is best; and if not, let the positive commandment come and override the negative commandment.\" According to this, on a woolen garment one must be required to make the white threads of wool as well.<br>Some say that there is indeed a dispute among the Amoraim about this, and the halakha follows Reish Lakish, that only on a linen garment must one place tekhelet threads of wool, since wool is intended for dyeing the tekhelet (Rambam, Ramban). And some say (Tosafot Yevamot 4b, s.v. \"de-afilu\"; Tur) that when the mitzva is fulfilled in its entirety with tekhelet, sha'atnez is permitted even without necessity; for example, on a linen garment the white threads too may be of wool. And some say (Rashba and Ritva) that Reish Lakish's intention is that if the owner of the linen garment can obtain white linen threads, he must place them on the garment and not use wool needlessly. But if he cannot, even though in principle they can be obtained, he is permitted to take white threads of wool, as Shmuel and Rav Yehuda said. (And there is an opinion in Tosafot there that there is no dispute among the Amoraim about this, but rather by Torah law there is no prohibition of sha'atnez in tzitzit, and rabbinically it is permitted only in a case of necessity.)<br>Even though by Torah law the mitzva of tzitzit overrides the prohibition of kil'ayim, in Jerusalem they did not place tekhelet on a linen garment, and Rebbi explained that it is a decree \"because they are not expert\" (Menaḥot 40a), lest they come to use kil'ayim at a time when there is no mitzva. According to the Rif, Rashi, Rambam, Ramban, Rashba and Rabbenu Yeruḥam, the meaning is that they did not place tekhelet made of wool, but only white cords. However, according to Rav Hai Gaon, Rav Amram Gaon, Rabbenu Tam, the Ittur, Semag and Semak, the Sages decreed not to place tzitzit on a linen garment at all, lest they place tekhelet on it and transgress the prohibition. As a matter of halakha, the SA and Rema (OḤ 9:6) ruled that ideally one should take into account the opinion of Rabbenu Tam and those who agree with him, and not make a tallit of linen. And one who has no woolen tallit had better fulfill the mitzva with a linen tallit and linen tzitzit, in accordance with the opinion of the Rif and those who agree with him."}]},{"slug":"17-05-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-05-11/","title_he":"יא – טעמי המצווה","title_en":"The Reasons for the Mitzva","body_en":"<p>The prohibitions of kil'ayim and sha'atnez are Divine statutes whose essence human reason cannot comprehend; nevertheless, the sages of the generations offered various reasons for the mitzva, the central one being that the Divine law is that wool and linen have different properties, and each kind has its own purpose, and God commanded that we give each kind its own distinct place and not mix them (Ramban, Vayikra 19:19).</p><p>Wool and linen are the two main kinds from which garments were made, and they are kinds that are different and utterly opposed. Wool is produced from the animal kingdom and alludes to the attribute of ḥesed (kindness); linen is produced from the plant kingdom and alludes to the attribute of din (strict judgment); and joining them is a complex and difficult matter. Therefore the Torah commanded joining them within the framework of a mitzva, in the priestly garments and in tzitzit, so that in this way their joining will be in holiness, and each force will be actualized in the proper way, adding blessing and life. Furthermore, since it is a mitzva to mix wool and linen in the priestly garments and in tzitzit, it is not proper for a person to use this special mixture for mundane matters, for it is intended for holiness alone (Da'at Zekenim, Devarim 22:11; Ḥizkuni, Vayikra 19:19; Metzudat David of the Radbaz 235; Likutei Halakhot, Kil'ei Begadim 1).</p><p>It was further said that only within the framework of a mitzva can the opposing forces be actualized properly, but not for the sake of a mitzva, their mixing causes the imprisonment of the good forces and prevents them from being actualized (Zohar III 86b). For within the framework of a mitzva, ḥesed and din find proper expression, but when they are mixed together, the evil inclination uses them for evil (Mikhtav Me-Eliyahu vol. 4, p. 176). For example, when a person needs to give tzedaka, it is convenient for the evil inclination to wrap itself in the attribute of din and argue that this poor person does not deserve tzedaka. And on the other hand, when one needs to stand up for the truth and save a person from the hand of a wicked man who is oppressing him, it is convenient for the evil inclination to wrap itself in the attribute of ḥesed and find merit in the wicked man, in order to be exempt from the obligation to rise to the aid of the oppressed.</p><p>One can say that through the separation between the two kinds that by their nature were the most suitable for making garments, and their joining within the framework of a mitzva, man learned to produce and create additional threads in which the human element that blends the natural properties is more prominent, and therefore blending them is easy and simple, and consequently involves neither prohibition nor mitzva.<sup>12</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"The Sages further said that the root of the evil in joining wool and linen is alluded to in the offerings of Kayin and Hevel, which caused the first murder, as it is stated (Bereshit 4:3-5, 8): \"And in the course of time Kayin brought of the fruit of the ground (flax) an offering to the Lord. And Hevel, he too brought of the firstlings of his flock (sheep) and of their fat (including the wool on the sheep); and the Lord had regard for Hevel and his offering, but for Kayin and his offering He had no regard; and Kayin was very angry, and his face fell … and Kayin rose up against Hevel his brother and killed him.\" Therefore we were commanded not to mix the offering of the righteous one with the offering of the wicked one (Tanḥuma Bereshit 9; Pirkei De-Rabbi Eliezer 21). One can further say that had Kayin offered his flax alone, his offering would have been accepted, for that was his level, as he was a tiller of the ground, and his offering was from what he grew. But after he saw Hevel offering a far finer offering, a sheep whose wool had not been shorn, he faced a difficult test: whether to wrap himself in humility and learn from his brother how to serve God in an enhanced manner, and perhaps even ask Hevel to be a kohen for him, or to envy his brother for having found more favor in God's eyes and murder him. In order to spare us such tests, the Torah commanded that we not mix these two kinds outside the framework of a mitzva (see Zohar III 87a)."}]}]});
